# Copy to deploy/.env on the Pi. Only needed when a default below is wrong.
# deploy/.env is gitignored; nothing here is a secret, and nothing that is one belongs here.

# Who owns the clone. The container writes sealed rounds into a bind-mounted checkout, so it runs
# as that user rather than as root — otherwise every file the loop writes is root-owned and the
# owner can no longer `git push` by hand. Defaults to 1000:1000, the first account on Raspberry Pi
# OS. Fill in from `id -u` and `id -g`.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000

# The clone to mount at /repo. The default is the parent of deploy/, which is right whenever
# compose is run from inside the clone — set it only if it is not.
# EPL_REPO=/home/pi/epl-predictor

# Where the deploy key lives. Mounted read-only; pushing is the whole of the container's business
# with it.
# SSH_DIR=/home/pi/.ssh

# --- The Telegram bot (issue #20) ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave these unset and the bot simply does not run: `python -m epl.bot notify` prints "not sending"
# and exits 0, and `serve` refuses to start. Nothing else in the project notices.
#
# Prefixed EPL_ on purpose. This Pi's other tenant is a paper-trading bot that reads
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN, and two bots on one box sharing a variable name is how this one comes up
# holding the other's token — which starts, polls, and posts a football scoreboard into a trading
# chat, having failed in a way that looks exactly like working.
#
# From @BotFather. This file is gitignored; a token in a tracked file is a token to revoke.
# EPL_TELEGRAM_TOKEN=123456789:AA...

# Comma-separated numeric Telegram user ids. Anyone not listed gets no answer at all — not a
# refusal, which would confirm both that the bot exists and that the id reached it. @userinfobot
# will tell you yours. An empty allowlist is refused rather than treated as "everyone".
# EPL_TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_IDS=123456789

# Who the push half writes to, if not everyone on the allowlist. Rarely needed.
# EPL_TELEGRAM_NOTIFY_IDS=123456789
